affiliation={Department of Fish, Wildlife, and Conservation Biology, Colorado State University, Fort Collins, CO  80523, United States; Department of Natural Resources and Environmental Science, University of Nevada, Reno, NV  89557, United States; U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service, Marine Mammals Management, Anchorage, AK  99503, United States; Southeast Alaska Inventory and Monitoring Network, National Park Service, Juneau, AK  99801, United States; Glacier Bay Field Station, National Park Service, Juneau, AK  99801, United States; U.S. Geological Survey, Colorado Cooperative Fish and Wildlife Research Unit, Fort Collins, CO  80523, United States; Department of Statistics, Colorado State University, Fort Collins, CO  80523, United States},
abstract={Optimal design procedures provide a framework to leverage the learning generated by ecological models to flexibly and efficiently deploy future monitoring efforts. At the same time, Bayesian hierarchical models have become widespread in ecology and offer a rich set of tools for ecological learning and inference. However, coupling these methods with an optimal design framework can become computationally intractable. Recursive Bayesian computation offers a way to substantially reduce this computational burden, making optimal design accessible for modern Bayesian ecological models. We demonstrate the application of so-called prior-proposal recursive Bayes to optimal design using a simulated data binary regression and the real-world example of monitoring and modeling sea otters in Glacier Bay, Alaska. These examples highlight the computational gains offered by recursive Bayesian methods and the tighter fusion of monitoring and science that those computational gains enable. © 2021 The Ecological Society of America. This article has been contributed to by US Government employees and their work is in the public domain in the USA.},
document_type={Article},
