affiliation={Department of Conservation Biology, Estación Biológica de Doñana – Consejo Superior de Investigaciones Científicas (EBD-CSIC), Américo Vespucio 26, Isla de la Cartuja, Seville, Andalucia  41092, Spain; Alnilam Research and Conservation, Pradillos 29, 28491 Navacerrada, Madrid, Spain; Departament de Biologia Evolutiva, Ecologia i Ciències Ambientals, Universitat de Barcelona, Facultat de Biologia, Av. Diagonal 643, Barcelona, Catalonia  08028, Spain; Laboratorio de SIG y Teledetección (LAST), Estación Biológica de Doñana – Consejo Superior de Investigaciones Científicas (EBD-CSIC), Américo Vespucio 26, Isla de la Cartuja, Seville, Andalucia  41092, Spain; Department of Ecology, Universidad Autónoma de Madrid, Madrid, Spain; Agencia de Medio Ambiente y Agua de Andalucía, Consejería de Medio Ambiente y Ordenación del Territorio, Junta de Andalucía, Johan Gütemberg, 1, Isla de la Cartuja, Seville, Andalucia  41092, Spain; Centro de Recuperación de Especies Marinas Amenazadas (CREMA), Aula del Mar de Málaga, Pacífico 80, Málaga, Andalucia  29004, Spain; Conservation, Information and Research on Cetaceans (CIRCE), Cabeza de Manzaneda 3, Algeciras-Pelayo, Cádiz, Andalucia  11390, Spain},
abstract={Co-occurring species are expected to distribute themselves unevenly throughout ecological niche dimensions to avoid competitive exclusion. However, few studies have previously investigated spatial and trophic factors structuring an entire cetacean community. Here, we combined density surface models (DSMs) with two dimension (δ15N and δ13C) isotopic niche spaces in order to identify the mechanistic processes underlying niche partitioning for the most abundant cetacean species inhabiting the Alboran Sea: the long-finned pilot whale (Globicephala melas), the Cuvier's beaked whale (Ziphius cavirostris), the Risso's dolphin (Grampus griseus), the bottlenose dolphin (Tursiops truncatus), the striped dolphin (Stenella coeruleoalba), and the short-beaked common dolphin (Delphinus delphis). DSMs provide a spatially-explicit assessment of species distribution through key spatial and environmental gradients, whereas isotopic niches characterize habitat and resource use. Our isotopic niche approach pointed to habitat and/or trophic segregation between the small (striped and short-beaked common dolphins) and large-sized cetacean species (Risso's and bottlenose dolphins, and long-finned pilot whales). Conversely, DSMs suggested a larger degree of spatial segregation among species by depth, with some overlap for offshore species (long-finned pilot, Cuvier's beaked whales and Risso's dolphins) and also between bottlenose and common dolphins. Thus, both components of the ecological niche apparently played an important role in explaining niche partitioning among species, which, in turn, might explain the high abundance and diversity of cetaceans in the Alboran Sea. Further, when both methodologies were applied in isolation, the structure and functioning of this cetacean community was poorly resolved. The combination of both approaches is therefore desirable when investigating niche partitioning among ecologically similar species within communities. © 2018 Elsevier Ltd},
author_keywords={Bottlenose dolphins;  Cuvier's beaked whales;  Delphinus delphis;  Ecological niche;  Globicephala melas;  Grampus griseus;  Long-finned pilot whales;  Risso's dolphin;  Short-beaked common dolphin;  Spatial distribution;  Stable isotopes;  Stenella coeruleoalba;  Striped dolphin;  Tursiops truncatus;  Ziphius cavirostris},
