pages={958-964},
note={cited By 0},
url={https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85055111986&partnerID=40&md5=c5f668956599a011b8b58d5899b4181b},
affiliation={Jacobs Group (Australia) Pty Ltd, Brisbane, QLD, Australia; NSW Department of Industry-Lands, Bega, New South Wales, Australia; NSW Department of Industry-Lands, Nowra, New South Wales, Australia},
abstract={The Port of Eden has seen increased activity from the cruise ship industry in recent years, however cruise ships are currently unable to berth alongside land based infrastructure in Snug Cove due to draft and length restrictions. Access for cruise ship passengers is via tender, contingent on favourable weather conditions. The New South Wales Department of Industry-Lands (DoI Lands) is seeking to extend the existing Breakwater Wharf at Snug Cove to provide access for cruise ships up to 325 m in length to berth safely. This will allow passengers to embark/disembark directly via the ship's gangway onto the wharf extension. The facility upgrade includes an extension of the existing Breakwater Wharf, installation of berthing and mooring dolphins, onshore bollards, and dredging of up to 231,500 m3. Given the length of such vessels, an area of approximately 190 m wide is to be dredged to allow for manoeuvring to and from the Breakwater Wharf berth. In the Environmental Impact Statement prepared for the project, dredging was identified as a project activity that has the potential to impact on the marine environment, in particular the sensitive environmental receptors located within Twofold Bay such as seagrass beds and mussel farms. To supplement the Environmental Impact Statement, DoI Lands commissioned Jacobs to assess the potential impacts of sediment plumes generated by dredging activities. Jacobs was also commissioned to assess the potential impacts of sediment plumes generated by propeller wash during the operation of the upgraded facility. This paper discusses the numerical modelling that was undertaken to simulate the potential sediment plumes resulting from these activities. It describes the 3-dimensional sediment plume model of Twofold Bay and surrounding portions of the Tasman Sea that was developed to simulate the key dispersion processes in the bay. The scenario modelling undertaken to assess the potential plumes generated by the proposed dredging and cruise vessel operations is also discussed, including the selection of appropriate input data to represent the likely source terms due to dredging activities and cruise vessel propeller wash. © Australasian Coasts and Ports 2017 Conference. All rights reserved.},
author_keywords={Dredge plume modelling;  Hydrodynamics;  Marine impact assessment;  Operational plume modelling;  Suspended sediments},
document_type={Conference Paper},
source={Scopus},
}

