abstract={Very large floating structure (VLFS) can be adopted to create floating airports, bridges, breakwaters, piers and docks, storage facilities (for oil & natural gas), etc. In this paper a dolphin-fender moored pontoon-type VLFS with dimensions of 180m × 80m ×16 m (length × width × depth) is studied both experimentally and numerically, focusing on the dynamic response of the structure due to regular waves. Three different shapes of VLFS with the same storage capacity, namely, normal pontoon (NP), pontoon with variable cross section (VP) and pontoon with slit walls (SP), are proposed and tested in order to achieve the most favourable platform configuration and mooring arrangement for reduced motion in sea environment. A series of parametric studies have been carried out by numerical analysis to predict the structural motions and fender force responses. In addition, this study has included model test results of 1:60 scale specimen platforms in a wave basin, which have been used to validate against the numerical simulation. The results validate the feasibility of using numerical simulation in the motion prediction of VLFS and the performance of dolphin-fender mooring system. Shape designs of the dolphin- fender moored VLFS that improve global performance and technical feasibility are discussed. Copyright © 2017 by MTEC 2017 Organizers.},
