abstract={Purpose: The aim of this article is to determine the feasibility of 3D planning software as a reliable resource for anatomy education, surgical training and computed-aided planning. Moreover, these programs are models of cooperative work with the interaction of maxillofacial surgeons, radiologists and engineers. Methodology: This article illustrates the components, system and clinical management of a new integrated computed program, Dolphin 3D Imaging® software. Results: We reviewed our experience using virtual surgery planning for maxillofacial surgeries. Dolphin 3D Imaging® was used in the treatment of major bone reconstructions, orthognathic surgery and TMJ surgery, and it was found to provide a good correlation between simulation and postoperative outcomes. Conclusions: Virtual surgery planning appears to improve the predictability of the planning and surgical outcomes. Nevertheless, further progress in the development of technologies for the acquisition of 3D image, new versions of software programs, and further studies of objective data are necessary to increase precision in computerized 3D planning.},
