affiliation={Laboratory of Applied Bioacoustics, Technical University of Catalonia, BarcelonaTech (UPC), Spain; SONSETC.com, Making Sense of Sounds, Barcelona, Spain},
abstract={Sound is increasing in the oceans and noise associated with offshore activities is suspect to having effects (physical, physiological and behavioral) on aquatic life such as marine mammals, reptiles, fish and invertebrates. These animals may be affected at various levels (physically or behaviorally) depending on the distance from the sound source. The challenge is to provide a real time acoustic monitoring system that provides both the operators and the regulators a means to better understand what sounds are being introduced to the environment and over what area these sounds propagate. In doing so, potential risks can be identified and better managed. During Oil and Gas E & P operations, the use of air guns has introduced additional artificial acoustic sources in the environment and underlined a need of detecting the presence of potential species at risk, e.g. cetaceans. This situation called for an increasing use of passive acoustics in areas of operations. Nevertheless, not only sound sources directly involved in geophysics (i.e. air guns) are playing a role in the technical capacity (or incapacity) to acoustically detect biological sounds above "background" noise: ship radiated noise from the streamer itself can mask frequency components commonly found in baleen whale calls, thus challenging their detection by conventional towed PAM systems. Here, we introduce a new approach to model and design E & P operations, based on 3D simulations of real-condition scenarios where a mesh of acoustic sensors are deployed around the operation area - be on drifting autonomous expandable buoys or unmanned vehicles, like wavegliders - communicate and automatically transmit in real-time the continuous detection, classification and localization data not only to the streamer itself but also to shore to allow effective mitigation actions to be taken. © 2014 SEG.},
document_type={Conference Paper},
source={Scopus},
}

