abstract={In 1982, Erich Lippmann patented a brilliant method to extend the frequency response of seismometers with an electromagnetic transducer. Adopted by Lennartz Electronic, this single-coil velocity-feedback strategy appears in a large part of the German company production. In case of strong signals from near events, seismometers using the Lippmann method may produce recordings with a characteristic 'whale-shape'. The article examines the Personal Simulation Program with Integrated Circuit Emphasis (PSPICE) model of an S13 seismometer treated with the Lippmann method, as well as a prototype built in INGV, and shows the effect of strong signals on them. © 2011 Springer Science+Business Media B.V.},
