affiliation={Applied Science Associates, Inc., 70 Dean Knauss Drive, Narragansett, RI  02882, United States},
abstract={SIMAP, ASA's Spill Impact MAPping model system, is designed to simulate the fate and effects of spilled oils and fuels in 3-dimensional space, allow evaluation of the effectiveness of spill response activities, and evaluate probabilities of trajectories and resulting impacts. It may be used for real-time spill simulation, contingency planning, and ecological risk assessments. Recently, Venezuela has been producing a new hydrocarbon-based fuel product called Orimulsion. Orimulsion is being used now in Canada, the UK, Denmark, and Japan. The fuel supplier is marketing the product in the U.S. with one power company presently applying for permits to utilize Orimulsion in its Manatee Plant outside Parrish Florida. Orimulsion is a mixture of 70% bitumen (a very heavy hydrocarbon fuel), surfactant, and water (about 30%). This emulsion partially mixes into the water column when it is spilled, as opposed to remaining as a surface slick as do oils. Thus, Orimulsion needs to be modeled by initializing as dispersed droplets (bitumen) in water. SIMAP was adapted to simulate Orimulsion spills. The bitumen in Orimulsion is initialized as a series of droplets of a range of sizes based on product specification data. The droplets are tracked through 3D space and time, in response to 3D time-varying currents and moving vertically in accordance with their buoyancy. The model simulates adsorption of and onto suspended sediment, resulting in sedimentation of material. Coalescence of droplets may also be evaluated. Larger droplets may surface as the result of this change in buoyancy and form slicks and tar balls. Dissolution of aromatics from the droplets is estimated. The fate and effects of aromatics in the water column and sediments are simulated. © 1995 Proceedings of the 18th AMOP Technical Seminar 1995. All rights reserved.},
document_type={Conference Paper},
source={Scopus},
}

